15 0 obj
<< /A 41 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating the relationship between peripheral inflammation and Alzheimer\220s disease via neuroinflammation, showing mechanisms such as peripheral immune cell infiltration, immune exosome vesicle communication, hormone translocation, vagal afferent reflex, inflammatory factor transcytosis, and soluble inflammatory substances. Surrounding icons represent immune cells, exosomes, neurons, hormones, and the brain.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 28 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Illustration depicting immune cell migration from blood vessels into tissue with a labeled key for cell types and soluble mediators; inset shows intracellular metabolism, signaling pathways \(glycolysis, TCA cycle\), and inflammatory mediators production.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 31 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating the gut-brain axis, showing intestinal microbes and metabolites in the gut lumen, transport across the intestinal barrier and bloodstream, and their contribution to neuroinflammation in the brain. Legend identifies cell and molecule types including probiotics, pathogenic bacteria, SCFAs, LPS, GABA, NLRP3, microglia, epithelial cells, and red blood cells. Arrows indicate the pathway from gut to brain.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Diagram showing a cross-section of a brain capillary with astrocytes, pericytes, endothelial cells, tight junctions, and a basement membrane. The right side illustrates three transport mechanisms across the blood-brain barrier: ATP-dependent carrier-mediated transport, paracellular leakage with barrier breakdown, and ATP-independent gradient-driven facilitated permeation, each labeling specific molecules, transporters, and structural proteins like ZO1, claudin, occludin, and V-cadherin.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
